  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N54895313199800652531">
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_geo"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Scarsdale, N.Y., has Tudor revivals and colonials line curved, hilly roads lined with Norway maples and rhododendron. There are 12 public parks, many ball fields and a nature preserve in six and a half square miles -- not counting the 700-acre Saxon Woods County Park, which takes up most of the southeastern corner. Schools are widely acclaimed, and town meetings can get heated. Commuters jam two Metro-North stations (Scarsdale and Hartsdale) on weekday mornings, though otherwise the town's vibe tends toward quiet.
Read More ...
To Do  |  Schools  |  Crime  |  Housing Stock  |  Who Lives There?  |  Walking Tour  |  Where Is It?</skos:definition>
