  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/9297659012232629181">
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/abkhazia_georgian_republic_geo"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated: July 20, 2009
Abkhazia, a region that lies along the eastern shore of the Black Sea, has been governed as a defacto protectorate of Russia since it broke away from Georgia in fighting that first erupted in 1989. After Russia routed Georgian forces in the area during a short war in August 2008, Moscow has recognized Abkhazia as an independent nation, a designation not recognized by most of the international community.
Abkhazians are a mix of Orthodox Christian and Muslims of the North Caucasian language family. They had an independent kingdom as early as the 8th century, and were later ruled by Turks and czars. The region became an autonomous Soviet republic in 1921 and was made part of the Georgian republic in 1930.</skos:definition>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://rdf.freebase.com/ns/en.abkhazia"/>
    <nyt:number_of_variants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#int">1</nyt:number_of_variants>
