    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://sws.geonames.org/1252558/"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Updated May 14, 2009
Stretching for about 1,800 miles from the Hindu Kush Mountains of Afghanistan east into northern Burma, the Himalayas form the youngest, highest and most formidable mountain range in the world. The peaks regularly top out above 26,000 feet, or 8,000 meters, nearly two miles higher than Mont Blanc, the highest point in Western Europe.
Underlying the vertiginous height and dynamism of the Himalayas is the nature of the tectonic plates that spawned them. All geological phenomena on Earth are caused by the movement of the two dozen or so crustal plates drifting across a partly molten mantle, and the Himalayas are the product of a collision between two continental plates.</skos:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
