  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/N55510146754222095371">
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/elements/nytd_geo"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.nytimes.com/greenpoint_nyc_geo"/>
    <nyt:latest_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2009-10-24</nyt:latest_use>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Greenpoint has historically been Polish and working-class â€” a humble, compact neighborhood in a corner pocket of Brooklyn. In recent years, the neighborhood has seen an influx of Hispanic families and a spillover of artist types and younger professionals from nearby Williamsburg. Trendy bars and boutiques have opened up. Rents, meanwhile, have remained low compared with those in Manhattan or brownstone Brooklyn, which makes Greenpoint an attractive alternative to living in a cramped East Village walkup.
Read More ...
To Do  |  Schools  |  Crime  |  Housing Stock  |  Who Lives There?  |  Walking Tour  |  Where Is It?</skos:definition>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Greenpoint (NYC)</skos:prefLabel>
