  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://data.nytimes.com/53259238663751762061">
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Cobble Hill (NYC)</skos:prefLabel>
    <geo:lat rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double">40.6875</geo:lat>
    <nyt:topicPage rdf:resource="http://topics.nytimes.com/top/realestate/neighborhood/ny/kings/brooklyn/cobble_hill/index.html"/>
    <nyt:first_use rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2008-03-30</nyt:first_use>
    <geo:long rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#double">-73.9980556</geo:long>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Cobble Hill has been a sleepy residential neighborhood since ferry service first connected the Brooklyn waterfront to Lower Manhattan in the 1830s. The area's first families -- mostly Italian dockworkers and industrial laborers -- made room for Middle Easterners in the early 20th century and, in more recent years, young families and professionals. These days the area's narrow, verdant blocks of historic row houses and adjacent commercial strips -- juxtaposing century-old butcher shops with designer clothing boutiques -- are considered among the most desirable in Brooklyn.
Read More ...
To Do  |  Schools  |  Crime  |  Housing Stock  |  Who Lives There?  |  Walking Tour  |  Where Is It?</skos:definition>
